 Installation and Environment Verification

The Lost and Found System application was extracted from the provided project archive (`grp05-lostandfound-main.zip`) and tested in a clean, dedicated local environment. The database installation, schema initialization, role configuration, and operational execution processes were conducted using an isolated PostgreSQL instance (grp05_lostandfounddb) to prevent unintended modifications to the primary development or production environments.

The system was evaluated based on PostgreSQL schema creation, table catalog verification, role-based access control (RBAC) initialization, privilege grant execution, operational query execution, and security restriction enforcement.



 Setup Notes and Technical Observations

During the database setup and testing process, specific environmental configurations were verified and managed:

1. Database Schema & Table Initialization: The database instance was successfully created as grp05_lostandfounddb on PostgreSQL 18.6. The schema inspection confirmed the creation of all eight (8) operational tables (categories, claim_images, claims, item_images, items, lost_report_images, lost_reports, and potential_matches), encompassing 55 individual column definitions and primary/foreign key constraints.

2. User Authentication & Role Management: Three group roles (app_runtime_grp, app_readonly_grp, and app_backup_grp) and two dedicated login accounts (lostandfound_app and lostandfound_readonly) were provisioned. During initial privilege testing, authentication for lostandfound_readonly required explicit password re-configuration (ALTER USER lostandfound_readonly WITH PASSWORD readonly123;) to establish connection sessions successfully.

3. Least-Privilege Enforcement: Object-level grants were applied across the public schema. Functional testing confirmed that app_readonly_grp maintains strict read-only (SELECT) permissions, while app_runtime_grp retains full DML access (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE). Unauthorized write operations (e.g., DELETE FROM categories) attempted under the read-only user properly triggered system permission rejections (ERROR: permission denied for table categories).



 Conclusion

The Lost and Found System database application successfully completed the documented local installation, schema inspection, and security verification procedures. The system demonstrated successful database initialization, role-based membership mapping, privilege catalog verification, authorized data retrieval, and active least-privilege access enforcement. The testing results confirm that the application and its underlying database infrastructure operate reliably in an isolated local environment when properly configured.

To streamline future group deployments, it is recommended to ensure standard database user credentials and role creation scripts are fully documented in the environment deployment guides.
